        "---\n",
        "title: \"Documents as Vectors\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Term-document matrices, TF-IDF, and cosine geometry\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "After text has been tokenized, the next classical step in NLP is to represent each document by a vector.  This chapter develops document vectors from a mathematical point of view.  We build count vectors, term-document matrices, normalized frequency vectors, TF-IDF vectors, and cosine similarity.  The goal is not only to compute these objects, but to understand what assumptions they encode and why they still matter in modern AI systems such as search engines and retrieval-augmented generation.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. define a document-vector representation as a map from token sequences to $R^d$;\n",
        "2. construct count vectors and document-term matrices from a corpus;\n",
        "3. distinguish counts, binary indicators, relative frequencies, and normalized vectors;\n",
        "4. explain TF-IDF as a mathematical weighting scheme for term specificity;\n",
        "5. compute cosine similarity and interpret it geometrically;\n",
        "6. rank documents in response to a query using sparse vector operations;\n",
        "7. describe the statistical assumptions behind bag-of-words models;\n",
        "8. implement document-vector methods in Python without relying on black-box NLP libraries;\n",
        "9. use interactive visualizations to study how TF-IDF and normalization change retrieval;\n",
        "10. use AI tools critically to test, explain, and improve vector-space representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why documents become vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "A document is naturally a sequence of tokens.  If the vocabulary is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V = \\{v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_d\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then a tokenized document has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(t_1,t_2,\\ldots,t_m), \\qquad t_k \\in V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Many mathematical and statistical methods, however, operate on vectors and matrices.  The basic idea of this chapter is to replace a token sequence by a vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi(x) \\in R^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where the $j$th coordinate measures how strongly token $v_j$ appears in the document.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Central idea.**  A document-vector model is a representation map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi:V^* \\to R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Different choices of $\\phi$ produce different mathematical models.  Count vectors, TF-IDF vectors, binary vectors, probability vectors, and neural embeddings are all representation maps.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "The simplest representation ignores word order and records only token counts.  Although this loses information, it produces a useful finite-dimensional object.  In classical NLP and information retrieval, this is called the **vector space model**.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Corpus, vocabulary, and notation\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D = (x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be a corpus of $n$ tokenized documents.  Each document $x_i$ is a finite sequence over the vocabulary $V$.\n",
        "\n",
        "We use the following notation throughout the chapter:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Symbol | Meaning |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| $n$ | number of documents |\n",
        "| $d$ | vocabulary size |\n",
        "| $x_i$ | document $i$ |\n",
        "| $v_j$ | vocabulary token $j$ |\n",
        "| $c_{ij}$ | count of token $v_j$ in document $x_i$ |\n",
        "| $X$ | document-term matrix |\n",
        "| $q$ | query vector |\n",
        "| $s_i$ | similarity score between document $i$ and query |\n",
        "\n",
        "The central matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X = (c_{ij}) \\in R^{n \\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c_{ij}=\\#\\{k : t_{ik}=v_j\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $t_{ik}$ is the $k$th token in document $x_i$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Row convention\n",
        "\n",
        "In this book we usually use the row convention:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_{i\\cdot} = \\text{vector representation of document } x_i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus each row is a document and each column is a term.  Some books use the transpose convention, where columns are documents.  Both are valid, but dimensions must be tracked carefully.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Dimension check.**  If $X \\in R^{n \\times d}$ and a query vector is $q \\in R^d$, then raw document-query scores have the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Xq \\in R^n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The output contains one score for each document.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Count vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "The count vector of a document $x$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c(x)=(c_1(x),c_2(x),\\ldots,c_d(x)) \\in N^d,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c_j(x)=\\#\\{k:t_k=v_j\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, suppose\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{\\text{data},\\text{language},\\text{learning},\\text{models},\\text{token}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "data data learning models\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "is the document, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c(x)=(2,0,1,1,0).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The count vector answers the question: **How many times does each vocabulary term appear?**\n",
        "\n",
        "### What counts preserve and lose\n",
        "\n",
        "The count vector preserves token frequency but discards order.  For example, the two documents\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "models predict language\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language predict models\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "have the same count vector.  Therefore, the count-vector map is not injective.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Bag-of-words assumption.**  A count vector treats a document as a multiset of tokens rather than as an ordered sequence.  Mathematically, this means the representation is invariant under permutations of the token positions.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "If $\\pi$ is a permutation of $\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$ and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x=(t_1,t_2,\\ldots,t_m),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then the permuted document\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi x=(t_{\\pi(1)},t_{\\pi(2)},\\ldots,t_{\\pi(m)})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "satisfies\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c(\\pi x)=c(x).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This invariance is both a strength and a weakness.  It makes the model simple and robust, but it cannot distinguish syntax, negation, or word order.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Binary vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Sometimes we care only whether a token appears, not how often.  The binary representation is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "b_j(x)=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1, & c_j(x)>0,\\\\\n",
        "0, & c_j(x)=0.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "b(x) \\in \\{0,1\\}^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Binary vectors are useful when repeated occurrences should not dominate.  For example, if one document repeats the word \"model\" many times, a raw count vector may make it appear overly similar to any query containing \"model.\"  Binary vectors reduce this effect.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Relative-frequency vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Raw counts depend strongly on document length.  If one document is twice as long as another, its count vector may have a larger norm simply because it contains more words.  A simple correction is to divide by the document length.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|x| = \\sum_{j=1}^d c_j(x)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the total number of vocabulary tokens in document $x$.  The relative-frequency vector is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_j(x)=\\frac{c_j(x)}{|x|}, \\qquad j=1,\\ldots,d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(x) \\in R^d, \\qquad p_j(x)\\ge 0, \\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j(x)=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus $p(x)$ is an empirical probability distribution over the vocabulary.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Statistical viewpoint.**  If a document is modeled as a sample from a multinomial distribution over $V$, then the relative-frequency vector is the maximum likelihood estimate of the token probability vector for that document.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This interpretation connects document vectors to statistics.  A document is not merely a string; it can be viewed as data generated by an unknown distribution on words.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The document-term matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "Given $n$ documents and $d$ vocabulary terms, the count vectors form a matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X =\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "--- & c(x_1)^T & ---\\\\\n",
        "--- & c(x_2)^T & ---\\\\\n",
        "& \\vdots & \\\\\n",
        "--- & c(x_n)^T & ---\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
        "\\in R^{n\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X_{ij}=c_j(x_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This matrix is usually sparse because most documents contain only a small fraction of all vocabulary terms.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the corpus\n",
        "\n",
        "1. `machine learning models learn from data`\n",
        "2. `language models predict the next token`\n",
        "3. `statistics studies data uncertainty and models`\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the vocabulary\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{\\text{data},\\text{language},\\text{learning},\\text{models},\\text{predict},\\text{statistics},\\text{token}\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "the document-term matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "1&0&1&1&0&0&0\\\\\n",
        "0&1&0&1&1&0&1\\\\\n",
        "1&0&0&1&0&1&0\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The first row says that the first document contains `data`, `learning`, and `models` once each.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: building a document-term matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "The following code builds a vocabulary and document-term matrix from scratch.  This is useful because it exposes the mathematical object directly."
      ],
