        "The output matrix is the basic object used by many classical NLP algorithms.  Notice that the matrix construction requires only tokenization, a vocabulary map, and counting.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Linear algebra viewpoint\n",
        "\n",
        "Once documents are represented as vectors, we can use linear algebra.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x,y \\in R^d\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be two document vectors.  We may compute:\n",
        "\n",
        "- their dot product $x^Ty$;\n",
        "- their norms $\\|x\\|_1$, $\\|x\\|_2$, or $\\|x\\|_\\infty$;\n",
        "- their angle through cosine similarity;\n",
        "- their distance, such as $\\|x-y\\|_2$;\n",
        "- their projection onto a low-dimensional subspace.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dot product is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x^Ty=\\sum_{j=1}^d x_jy_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For count vectors, this quantity is large when the two documents share many terms with high counts.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, dot products are sensitive to length.  A longer document tends to have a larger norm and may receive a large dot product even when it is not especially similar in content.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Cosine similarity\n",
        "\n",
        "Cosine similarity normalizes by vector length:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{cos}(x,y)=\\frac{x^Ty}{\\|x\\|_2\\|y\\|_2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "provided $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Geometrically, this is the cosine of the angle between $x$ and $y$.  It measures direction rather than magnitude.  If $x$ and $y$ point in the same direction, then their cosine similarity is $1$.  If they are orthogonal, the similarity is $0$.\n",
        "\n",
        "For nonnegative document vectors, cosine similarity lies between $0$ and $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Scale invariance.**  For any $a>0$ and $b>0$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{cos}(ax,by)=\\operatorname{cos}(x,y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, cosine similarity ignores positive rescaling of documents.  This is useful when document lengths differ.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Matrix form of cosine retrieval\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose $X\\in R^{n\\times d}$ contains document vectors and $q\\in R^d$ is a query vector.  The raw dot-product scores are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "r=Xq \\in R^n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The cosine scores are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_i=\\frac{X_{i\\cdot}q}{\\|X_{i\\cdot}\\|_2\\|q\\|_2}, \\qquad i=1,\\ldots,n.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If we define the row-normalized matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widetilde X_{i\\cdot}=\\frac{X_{i\\cdot}}{\\|X_{i\\cdot}\\|_2}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the normalized query\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widetilde q=\\frac{q}{\\|q\\|_2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s=\\widetilde X\\widetilde q.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus retrieval by cosine similarity is matrix-vector multiplication after normalization.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: cosine search"
