        "This code shows that document retrieval can be written as a small linear algebra problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Term frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "Raw counts can be transformed before similarity is computed.  The first transformation is **term frequency**.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple term frequency is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{tf}_{ij}=c_{ij}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A length-normalized term frequency is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{tf}_{ij}=\\frac{c_{ij}}{\\sum_{k=1}^d c_{ik}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A log-scaled term frequency is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{tf}_{ij}=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1+\\log(c_{ij}), & c_{ij}>0,\\\\\n",
        "0, & c_{ij}=0.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log transform reduces the effect of repeated occurrences.  For example, a term appearing ten times should not always be ten times as important as a term appearing once.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Document frequency and inverse document frequency\n",
        "\n",
        "Some words appear in many documents.  These common terms often have low discriminative value.  For example, words such as `the`, `and`, `model`, or `data` may appear frequently in a technical corpus.\n",
        "\n",
        "The document frequency of token $v_j$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{df}_j=\\#\\{i:c_{ij}>0\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "It counts how many documents contain the term at least once.\n",
        "\n",
        "The inverse document frequency is commonly defined as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{idf}_j=\\log\\left(\\frac{n+1}{\\operatorname{df}_j+1}\\right)+1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The added $1$ in numerator and denominator is smoothing.  It avoids division by zero and keeps the weight finite.  Other conventions are also used.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Interpretation.**  If $\\operatorname{df}_j/n$ is the empirical probability that a random document contains token $v_j$, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{n}{\\operatorname{df}_j}\\right)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is the negative logarithm of this empirical probability.  Rare terms receive larger weights because they are more informative for distinguishing documents.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## TF-IDF vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "The TF-IDF weight of term $v_j$ in document $x_i$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_{ij}=\\operatorname{tf}_{ij}\\operatorname{idf}_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In matrix form, if $T=(\\operatorname{tf}_{ij})$ and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "D_{\\operatorname{idf}}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\operatorname{idf}_1,\\ldots,\\operatorname{idf}_d),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then the TF-IDF matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W=TD_{\\operatorname{idf}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a column weighting of the term-frequency matrix.  Terms that appear in many documents get smaller weights.  Terms that appear in fewer documents get larger weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why TF-IDF is useful\n",
        "\n",
        "TF-IDF balances two effects:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Within-document importance.**  A term is important if it appears often in a particular document.\n",
        "2. **Across-document specificity.**  A term is more informative if it does not appear in almost every document.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus TF-IDF is a simple way to say:\n",
        "\n",
        "> A good keyword should be frequent in this document but not too frequent across the whole corpus.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: TF-IDF from scratch"
