        "The resulting matrix has the same shape as the count matrix, but its entries have changed.  Common tokens receive smaller column weights, while more specific tokens receive larger column weights.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Comparing count vectors and TF-IDF vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Count vectors often reward documents that share common terms with the query.  TF-IDF vectors reward documents that share more specific terms.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the query is\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language token prediction\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A count-vector search might rank a document highly because it contains `language` and `token`.  TF-IDF may increase the importance of `token` if it appears in only a few documents.\n",
        "\n",
        "This distinction matters in academic settings.  For example, in a corpus of course descriptions, words such as `course`, `students`, and `methods` may appear everywhere.  Words such as `Bayesian`, `topology`, or `transformer` may be more specific.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interactive HTML: TF-IDF and cosine retrieval explorer\n",
        "\n",
        "The following interactive component lets you adjust the IDF strength and choose a query.  It uses a tiny corpus so that the mathematics remains visible.  Move the slider to see how document rankings change.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-format=\"html\"}\n",
        "<div class=\"interactive-card\">\n",
        "  <h3>Interactive TF-IDF retrieval explorer</h3>\n",
        "  <p>\n",
        "    Choose a query and adjust the IDF strength.  When the IDF strength is 0, the system behaves like normalized term frequency.  When it is 1, it uses the full smoothed IDF weight.\n",
        "  </p>\n",
        "\n",
