        "\n",
        "## The geometry of sparse document vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Document vectors often live in very high dimensions.  A vocabulary may contain thousands or millions of tokens, while a single document uses only a small subset.  Therefore, most entries of a document vector are zero.\n",
        "\n",
        "This creates a sparse geometry:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\#\\{j:x_j\\ne 0\\} \\ll d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Sparse vectors are computationally useful because we do not need to store or multiply by all zero entries.  They are also geometrically interesting.  Two randomly chosen short documents may have no terms in common, which makes their dot product zero.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Orthogonality in sparse spaces\n",
        "\n",
        "If two count vectors have disjoint supports, then\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "x^Ty=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For nonnegative vectors, this means their cosine similarity is zero.  In a large vocabulary, many pairs of documents may be nearly orthogonal even if they are topically related but use different words.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, the documents\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "neural network training\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "deep learning optimization\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "are semantically related, but a simple bag-of-words model may give low similarity if the exact words do not overlap.  This limitation motivates later chapters on latent semantic analysis, word embeddings, and transformer representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Stop words and projection\n",
        "\n",
        "A common preprocessing step removes very frequent words such as `the`, `and`, `of`, and `to`.  Mathematically, stop-word removal is a coordinate projection.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $S\\subseteq \\{1,2,\\ldots,d\\}$ be the set of coordinates corresponding to terms we keep.  The projection map is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_S:R^d\\to R^{|S|},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_S(x)=(x_j)_{j\\in S}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This map discards coordinates.  It can improve retrieval by removing noise, but it can also remove meaningful words in some contexts.  For example, `not` is often a stop word in generic lists, but it is crucial for sentiment analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Modeling warning.**  Stop-word removal is not universally safe.  It is a projection that may remove coordinates relevant to the task.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Statistical model behind bag-of-words\n",
        "\n",
        "The bag-of-words representation can be derived from a multinomial model.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose document $x_i$ has length $m_i$ and is generated by independent draws from a categorical distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_i=(\\theta_{i1},\\ldots,\\theta_{id}),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\theta_{ij}\\ge 0, \\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^d \\theta_{ij}=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The probability of observing counts\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c_i=(c_{i1},\\ldots,c_{id})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(c_i\\mid \\theta_i)=\\frac{m_i!}{c_{i1}!c_{i2}!\\cdots c_{id}!}\\prod_{j=1}^d \\theta_{ij}^{c_{ij}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood, ignoring the multinomial coefficient, is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\theta_i)=\\sum_{j=1}^d c_{ij}\\log \\theta_{ij}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Maximizing this subject to $\\sum_j \\theta_{ij}=1$ gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat\\theta_{ij}=\\frac{c_{ij}}{m_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the relative-frequency vector is the maximum likelihood estimate of the document's token distribution under this simplified model.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What the model assumes\n",
        "\n",
        "The multinomial bag-of-words model assumes:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. token order is irrelevant;\n",
        "2. tokens are conditionally independent given $\\theta_i$;\n",
        "3. each document has its own word distribution;\n",
        "4. dependence between neighboring words is ignored.\n",
        "\n",
        "These assumptions are false for real language, but they give a mathematically transparent baseline.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Connection to classification\n",
        "\n",
        "Document vectors are not only used for retrieval.  They can also be used for classification.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose each document has a label\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "y_i\\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,K\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A linear classifier has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f(x_i)=\\arg\\max_{k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}} \\left(w_k^T\\phi(x_i)+b_k\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $\\phi(x_i)$ may be a count vector, TF-IDF vector, or another document representation.  The same vectorization step supports logistic regression, support vector machines, naive Bayes, and neural networks.\n",
        "\n",
        "For binary classification, a common model is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(y_i=1\\mid x_i)=\\sigma(w^T\\phi(x_i)+b),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sigma(z)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This shows how vector-space NLP connects directly to statistics and machine learning.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Low-dimensional visualization by SVD\n",
        "\n",
        "Because document vectors are high-dimensional, we often visualize them by projecting to two dimensions.  A simple method is singular value decomposition.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $W\\in R^{n\\times d}$ be a centered or weighted document-term matrix.  Its singular value decomposition is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W=U\\Sigma V^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The best rank-$k$ approximation in Frobenius norm is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_k=U_k\\Sigma_kV_k^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For visualization, we often use the rows of\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "U_2\\Sigma_2\\in R^{n\\times 2}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "as two-dimensional document coordinates.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Preview.**  This idea leads to latent semantic analysis, which studies low-rank structure in document-term matrices.  Chapter 7 returns to this topic in detail.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: two-dimensional document map"
