        "This visualization is not a replacement for the full high-dimensional representation.  It is a diagnostic tool that helps us inspect whether documents cluster by topic or vocabulary.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Evaluation of retrieval\n",
        "\n",
        "A document-vector method is often used for retrieval: given a query, return a ranked list of documents.  To evaluate retrieval, we need relevance judgments.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a query $q$, let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "R(q)\\subseteq \\{1,2,\\ldots,n\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the set of relevant documents.  If a system returns the top $k$ documents\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "A_k(q),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then precision at $k$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Precision@}k=\\frac{|A_k(q)\\cap R(q)|}{k}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall at $k$ is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Recall@}k=\\frac{|A_k(q)\\cap R(q)|}{|R(q)|}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision measures how many retrieved documents are relevant.  Recall measures how many relevant documents were retrieved.\n",
        "\n",
        "For course search, advising chatbots, and RAG systems, retrieval evaluation is essential.  A language model can only answer from useful evidence if the retrieval step finds useful evidence.\n",
        "\n",
        "## AI component: document vectors in RAG systems\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern retrieval-augmented generation systems often use neural embeddings rather than TF-IDF.  However, the mathematical pipeline is similar:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{document} \\xrightarrow{\\phi} R^p,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\text{query} \\xrightarrow{\\phi} R^p,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\text{rank by similarity}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In classical vector search, $\\phi$ may be TF-IDF.  In neural vector search, $\\phi$ may be a transformer embedding model.  The retrieval step still depends on geometry: dot products, cosine similarity, nearest neighbors, and ranking.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.ai-box}\n",
        "**AI-assisted learning activity.**  Ask an AI system:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Give three short documents for which TF-IDF search succeeds and three short documents for which TF-IDF search fails.  For each failure, explain the mathematical reason in terms of vocabulary overlap, synonymy, negation, or word order.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then verify the examples by computing count vectors and cosine similarities yourself.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## AI component: auditing a representation\n",
        "\n",
        "When using AI tools to help build NLP systems, ask the tool to make the representation assumptions explicit.  A useful prompt is:\n",
        "\n",
        "> I am representing documents using TF-IDF vectors.  List the mathematical assumptions of this representation.  Which assumptions are problematic for sentiment analysis, course advising, and mathematical text search?\n",
        "\n",
        "A good answer should mention at least:\n",
        "\n",
        "- loss of word order;\n",
        "- dependence on exact vocabulary overlap;\n",
        "- sensitivity to tokenization;\n",
        "- inability to understand synonyms unless they share terms;\n",
        "- possible removal of important stop words;\n",
        "- limited handling of negation;\n",
        "- dependence on the corpus used to compute IDF.\n",
        "\n",
        "Use AI tools as mathematical critics, not as replacements for mathematical verification.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Worked example: hand calculation\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider three documents with vocabulary\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{a,b,c,d\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the count matrix is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "2&1&0&0\\\\\n",
        "1&0&2&0\\\\\n",
        "0&1&0&3\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let the query be\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "q=(1,1,0,0)^T.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The raw dot-product scores are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Xq=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "3\\\\\n",
        "1\\\\\n",
        "1\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The Euclidean norms are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\|q\\|_2=\\sqrt{2},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\|X_{1\\cdot}\\|_2=\\sqrt{5}, \\qquad\n",
        "\\|X_{2\\cdot}\\|_2=\\sqrt{5}, \\qquad\n",
        "\\|X_{3\\cdot}\\|_2=\\sqrt{10}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus the cosine scores are\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_1=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_2=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "s_3=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{20}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, document 1 is most similar to the query.\n",
        "\n",
        "This small example illustrates the full retrieval calculation: vectorize, multiply, normalize, rank.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Common pitfalls\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pitfall 1: confusing rows and columns\n",
        "\n",
        "Always state whether documents are rows or columns.  In this book, documents are rows.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pitfall 2: forgetting zero vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "Cosine similarity is undefined when one vector has zero norm.  This can happen if a query contains no vocabulary terms.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pitfall 3: assuming TF-IDF understands meaning\n",
        "\n",
        "TF-IDF is a lexical method.  It does not know that `doctor` and `physician` are related unless the corpus and vocabulary create indirect associations.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pitfall 4: ignoring corpus dependence\n",
        "\n",
        "IDF weights depend on the corpus.  If the corpus changes, the IDF weights change.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Pitfall 5: treating preprocessing as neutral\n",
        "\n",
        "Lowercasing, punctuation removal, stop-word removal, and stemming all change the mathematical representation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Chapter summary\n",
        "\n",
        "The main construction in this chapter is the document-vector map\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\phi:V^*\\to R^d.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The simplest choice is the count vector.  From count vectors we build the document-term matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "X\\in R^{n\\times d}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We then transform this matrix using normalization and TF-IDF weighting.  Similarity between documents or between a query and documents is often measured by cosine similarity:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{cos}(x,y)=\\frac{x^Ty}{\\|x\\|_2\\|y\\|_2}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These ideas form the classical foundation of search and retrieval.  They also prepare us for modern NLP, where documents and queries are represented by learned dense vectors instead of sparse TF-IDF vectors.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exercises\n",
        "\n",
        "### Conceptual exercises\n",
